                <div class="alert alert-warning">
                    <i class="fas fa-traffic-light me-2"></i> <strong>The Traffic Light Story:</strong> When you drive, traffic lights tell you what to do. Green means go (Success). Red means stop (Error). Yellow means caution (Redirect). APIs use <strong>Status Codes</strong> exactly like traffic lights to tell the client what happened!
                </div>

                <h2>Connecting to C#</h2>
                <p>When you write a C# method, it usually returns a value. But what if it fails? You might throw an exception: <code>throw new Exception("Not found");</code></p>
                <p>In REST APIs, we don't just throw exceptions. We return a specific HTTP Status Code so the client knows exactly what went wrong without crashing.</p>

                <h2>Common Status Codes (Cheat Sheet)</h2>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>200 OK:</strong> The request was successful.</li>
                    <li><strong>201 Created:</strong> A new resource was successfully created (used after POST).</li>
                    <li><strong>400 Bad Request:</strong> The client sent invalid data.</li>
                    <li><strong>401 Unauthorized:</strong> You need to log in.</li>
                    <li><strong>404 Not Found:</strong> The URL or data doesn't exist.</li>
                    <li><strong>500 Internal Server Error:</strong> The server crashed (a bug in your C# code).</li>
                </ul>

                <h2>Example: Returning Status Codes in ASP.NET Core</h2>
                <p>ASP.NET Core provides built-in helper methods to return these codes:</p>
<pre><code>[HttpGet("{id}")]
public IActionResult GetUser(int id)
{
    var user = _database.Users.Find(id);

    if (user == null)
    {
        // Returns HTTP 404
        return NotFound("User does not exist"); 
    }

    if (id < 0)
    {
        // Returns HTTP 400
        return BadRequest("ID cannot be negative"); 
    }

    // Returns HTTP 200 with the user object
    return Ok(user); 
}</code></pre>

                <h2>Handling Server Errors (Try-Catch)</h2>
<pre><code>[HttpPost]
public IActionResult CreateUser(User user)
{
    try
    {
        _database.Users.Add(user);
        _database.SaveChanges();
        return CreatedAtAction(nameof(GetUser), new { id = user.Id }, user);
    }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        // Log the error, and return 500
        return StatusCode(500, "An internal server error occurred.");
    }
}</code></pre>

